# Food Order App

## Project Conventions

- Stack: Next.js 16 App Router, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS 4, MongoDB (Mongoose), NextAuth, Stripe, Cloudinary, Leaflet, Resend, Upstash Redis, date-fns, TanStack Query, nuqs, cmdk, Vercel Web Analytics, Sentry.
- Prefer small, incremental changes.
- Before implementing a new feature or non-trivial app logic, create or switch to a dedicated local feature branch from `main` unless the user explicitly asks to work on the current branch.
- Follow existing patterns in `app/`, `libs/`, `models/`, `components/`, and `contexts/`.
- Avoid introducing new dependencies unless explicitly requested.

## API And Data Rules

- Validate request input in route handlers.
- Keep API response shapes backward compatible unless change is requested.
- Use existing models in `models/` for data access.
- Ask before introducing schema migrations or destructive data operations.
- Store timestamps as MongoDB `Date` values, return ISO/raw date fields from APIs, and format user-facing dates through `libs/dateFormat.ts`.
- Preserve restaurant accepting-order logic in checkout: working hours, the 60-minute-before-closing cutoff, pause state, blocked dates, delivery radius, paid active kitchen orders, item availability, coupons, and loyalty must be checked before Stripe sessions are created.
- Treat best coupon suggestions as UI help only; checkout must revalidate coupons server-side.
- Reorder flows must rebuild from current `menu_items` data and block deleted, unavailable, cross-restaurant, or invalid items.
- Preserve order timing fields and snapshots for preparation/delivery estimates and timeline UI.
- Preserve Upstash Redis rate limits on credentials login, register, forgot password, resend verification, checkout, support ticket creation, and AI menu description generation.
- Use TanStack Query for client-side server state that needs cache, refetch, invalidation, optimistic updates, or polling. Keep message inbox/thread views, favorite ID/list views, and shared keys in `libs/queryKeys.ts`.
- `NuqsAdapter` is mounted in `app/layout.tsx`; use `nuqs` for URL-backed filters, sorting, search, selected ticket links, report periods, and pagination when users should be able to refresh or share the same view.

## Security And Integrations

- Never expose secrets to client code.
- Keep Stripe, Cloudinary, and Resend keys in server-only code.
- Preserve role checks for admin, courier, and user flows.
- Preserve delivery double confirmation: courier PIN handoff first, then customer or restaurant admin final completion.
- Keep support ticket visibility scoped by role and target: restaurant support to restaurant owners, app support to super admin.
- Keep ETA-style notifications and late active-order alerts aligned with order timeline state; failed-delivery review notifications should route restaurant admins to `/admin-dashboard/orders/[id]`.
- Keep SSE realtime updates lightweight and role-scoped. Use streams as refresh signals, keep polling fallback, and read final data from existing JSON APIs.
- Prefer invalidating TanStack Query keys from SSE handlers instead of manually duplicating cached server state.
- `components/shared/AppCommandPalette.tsx` uses `cmdk`; add important new routes/actions there when adding major navigation surfaces.
- `components/shared/AppErrorBoundary.tsx` uses `react-error-boundary` and reports caught client render errors to Sentry; keep it for client recovery, not API validation.
- Keep Sentry instrumentation files intact. `NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN` is public, but `SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN` is a build-time secret for source maps and must never enter client code.
- Keep Stripe webhook handling idempotent.

## High-Risk Areas

- Auth: `libs/authOptions.ts`, `app/api/auth/**`
- Payments: `app/api/checkout/**`, `app/api/payment-link/**`, `app/api/webhook/**`
- Email: `libs/sendPurchaseReceiptEmail.tsx`, `components/resend/**`
- Courier tracking: `app/my-delivery/**`, `app/my-deliveries/**`, `app/api/my-delivery/**`, `app/api/my-deliveries/**`
- Order operations: `app/api/checkout/**`, `app/api/orders/**`, `app/api/support-tickets/**`, `components/shared/OrderPhaseTimeline.tsx`

## Validation Checklist

- Run `npm run lint` after meaningful edits.
- Run `npm run test` when changing business logic in auth, checkout, webhook, order, or courier flows.
- If env usage changes, update `example.env`.
- For major feature additions, update `README.md` and root AI docs.

## Testing Conventions

- Use Vitest for TypeScript tests.
- Keep tests in `__tests__/` grouped by domain.
- Keep e2e tests in `e2e/` for real workflow validation.
- Keep reusable fixtures in `mocks/`.
- Prefer behavior-driven assertions over implementation-coupled assertions.
- Add both success and failure scenarios for route handlers and auth callbacks.
- For order-flow work, cover checkout validation, coupon suggestion, restaurant availability, notification copy, courier summaries, and lifecycle transitions.
- Ensure e2e tests clean up created records and use `MONGODB_URL_TESTS`.
